234375, -0.023040771484375, 0.0078277587890625, 0.017974853515625, 0.016815185546875, -0.035125732421875, -0.017730712890625, 0.0277557373046875, -0.0013074874877929688, 0.0185546875, -0.0227813720703125, 0.033966064453125, 0.0516357421875, 0.03497314453125, 0.01049041748046875, -0.00627899169921875, -0.01424407958984375, 0.0124969482421875, 0.047332763671875, -0.0357666015625, -0.0024242401123046875, -0.056671142578125, -0.058074951171875, 0.0167999267578125, -0.0087127685546875, 0.002620697021484375, -0.00701904296875, 0.00439453125, -0.00801849365234375, 0.0119171142578125, -0.006534576416015625, -0.016387939453125, -0.006195068359375, 0.00977325439453125, 0.0024967193603515625, 0.0178070068359375, -0.011444091796875, -0.003459930419921875, -0.003040313720703125, 0.0200042724609375, 0.0033721923828125, 0.0015363693237304688, 0.01031494140625, -0.0020122528076171875, 0.0087432861328125, -0.0222320556640625, 0.00928497314453125, 0.016265869140625, -0.013336181640625, 0.006084442138671875, -0.0034885406494140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, implementations based on in-house small language models (SLMs) offer potential advantages in cost, data security, confidentiality, and interpretability.\n\n**Objective, Novelty, and Research Questions:**  \nThe main objective of this study is to evaluate the effectiveness of in-house SLMs for invoice categorisation, with a focus on understanding how the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings relates to classification performance. Our work is novel in extending prior token-level findings on embedding geometry to sentence embeddings within a financial context and in demonstrating the practical viability of SLMs for client-specific invoice classification.  \nTo guide our investigation, we address the following research questions:  \n1. Can in-house SLMs, fine-tuned on client-specific data, achieve competitive performance in invoice-to-GL code classification compared to established baselines?  \n2. How does the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings (e.g., anisotropy, clustering) relate to classification performance, particularly with respect to vendor identity?  \n3. Does augmenting invoice text with structured input (explicit field labels) improve SLM performance?  \n4. What are the practical implications of embedding geometry for model deployment and improvement in real-world financial settings?\n\n**Background and Definitions:**  \nWe analyse the pre-trained embedding geometry of a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa). In this context, \"anisotropic\" refers to the embedding space being unevenly distributed, with certain directions or regions more densely populated, while \"isotropic clusters\" are locally uniform groupings where embeddings are distributed more evenly in all directions. We find that the sentence-embedding space of our financial corpus is globally anisotropic but composed of locally isotropic clusters, and these clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity.\n\n**Dataset Composition:**  \nOur dataset consists of approximately 10,000 anonymised invoices sourced from 12 distinct clients, spanning sectors such as retail, manufacturing, and professional services. Each invoice is labelled with the correct GL code (from a set of 45 unique codes, covering categories such as office supplies, travel expenses, consulting fees, and utilities) and vendor identity. The invoices vary in length (median: 120 words, range: 30\u2013500 words) and format, and represent a mix of recurring and one-off purchases. The dataset was curated to ensure diversity in both invoice types and client business models.\n\n**Methods:**  \nPreprocessing involved standardising invoice text, removing personally identifiable information, and tokenising using the default SBERT and DeBERTa pipelines. For model training, we fine-tuned SBERT (distiluse-base-multilingual-cased-v2) on a single GPU for 5 epochs, with a batch size of 32 and a learning rate of 2e-5. The train-test split was stratified by client to ensure that test invoices from each client were not seen during training, supporting assessment of generalisability.  \nWe compared SBERT\u2019s performance to two baselines:  \n1. **Zero-shot LLM baseline:** We used OpenAI\u2019s GPT-3.5-turbo, prompting it with the raw invoice text and a list of possible GL codes, asking it to select the most appropriate code. Prompts were standardised and inference was performed with temperature set to 0 for deterministic outputs.  \n2. **Vendor identity classifier:** This baseline assigns the most frequent GL code for each vendor, reflecting a simple heuristic based on historical patterns.  \nThese baselines were chosen to represent (a) a state-of-the-art general-purpose LLM without fine-tuning, and (b) a practical rule-based approach commonly used in industry.  \nEvaluation metrics included accuracy and F1 score, reported separately for each experiment.\n\n**Results:**  \nSBERT fine-tuned on our dataset achieved 0.96 accuracy (95% CI: 0.95\u20130.97) and 0.93 F1 score (95% CI: 0.92\u20130.94) on invoice classification, outperforming both baselines. The zero-shot LLM baseline achieved 0.85 accuracy (95% CI: 0.83\u20130.87) and 0.81 F1 (95% CI: 0.79\u20130.83), while the vendor identity baseline reached 0.88 accuracy (95% CI: 0.86\u20130.89) and 0.83 F1 (95% CI: 0.81\u20130.85).  \nPaired bootstrap resampling (n=1000) confirmed that SBERT\u2019s improvements over both baselines are statistically significant (p < 0.01). Notably, SBERT showed improved performance for smaller, challenging categories (F1 improvement of 0.08 over LLM baseline for categories with <100 examples) and for new clients. For the generalisation task\u2014classifying invoices for new clients with limited data\u2014SBERT achieved 0.90 F1 (95% CI: 0.88\u20130.92) with roughly 100 client-specific invoices.\n\n**Embedding Geometry and Structured Input:**  \nOur geometric analysis revealed that pre-trained embedding geometry is associated with classification performance: invoices from the same vendor tend to cluster together in the embedding space, facilitating accurate categorisation. Quantitatively, we measured cluster purity (mean: 0.81) and silhouette score (mean: 0.62) for vendor-based clusters in the SBERT embedding space. t-SNE visualisations (see Supplementary Figure 1) further illustrate the presence of locally isotropic clusters corresponding to vendors within a globally anisotropic space.  \nWe also tested the effect of \"structured input,\" defined as augmenting invoice text with explicit field labels (e.g., \"Vendor: X; Amount: Y; Description: Z\") to mimic the way a human would parse information. Contrary to expectations, this structured input did not improve SLM performance (\u0394F1 < 0.01), suggesting that the model already captures relevant structure from the raw text.\n\n**Discussion, Limitations, and Future Work:**  \nThese findings indicate that in-house SLMs are a promising solution for invoice categorisation, offering strong performance with relatively small, client-specific datasets. However, our study is limited by the size and diversity of the dataset, the predominance of English-language invoices, and potential biases such as overrepresentation of frequent vendors and shorter invoices. These biases may affect generalisability to less common vendors or atypical invoice formats.  \nEthical considerations include ensuring data privacy during model training and deployment, as well as monitoring for fairness across vendors and clients to avoid perpetuating historical biases in GL code assignment.  \nUnderstanding embedding geometry has practical implications: the observed vendor-based clustering suggests that incorporating vendor information or designing models to exploit local isotropy could further improve classification, especially for new or rare vendors. Additionally, geometric insights may inform strategies for anomaly detection, interpretability, and robust handling of out-of-distribution invoices.  \nFuture work should explore larger and more varied datasets, multilingual settings, and further investigate why structured input does not benefit SLMs. Additionally, interpretability and robustness to adversarial or out-of-distribution invoices remain important areas for further research."}}